<header>
  <p class="eyebrow">Adversarial review · independent reader</p>
  <h1>Seventeen findings on the draft README</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">The draft was read against five standards: exhibition catalog, not
  OSS pitch; humble custodianship, zero self-credit; timeless formulation; no meta
  commentary; factual precision against primary sources. Findings are ordered by
  severity.</p>
</header>

<p class="verification"><strong>Verification notes.</strong> All linked paths spot-checked
exist (<code>Specification-1.0-frozen/fortress.1.0.pdf</code>,
<code>Papers/Types/journal/</code>, <code>Papers/Dispatch/SteelePOPL2011.pdf</code>,
<code>Papers/Welterweight/</code>, <code>Papers/RuntimeInstantiation/</code>,
<code>Papers/Implementation/FortressEncodings-rendered.pdf</code>,
<code>research/extracts/SteeleJuliaCon2016-extract.md</code>,
<code>ProjectFortress/demos/buffons.fss</code>, <code>LICENSE</code>). Paper titles
verified against their <code>\title{}</code> lines. Several numbers and dates verified
against the tree and git history — with the discrepancies below.</p>

<h2>Findings, by severity</h2>

<ol class="findings">

<li><span class="num">1</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">False claim: rung tags do not exist.</p>
<blockquote>each rung gated on the complete original test suite and tagged, so you can
check out whichever era of the Java ecosystem you want to explore.</blockquote>
<p><code>git tag</code> lists exactly one tag, <code>1.0</code>, pointing at a March 2008
commit. There are no modernization-rung tags. This is the README's most immediately
falsifiable sentence (standard 5), and its "so you can…" invitation is also
adoption-pitch register (standard 1).</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> either create the rung tags before publishing, or
delete the clause entirely.</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">2</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">The modernization paragraph is self-congratulation in slow motion.</p>
<blockquote>modernized in small, individually tested steps — a ladder from JDK 8 up to
current JDKs, each rung gated on the complete original test suite</blockquote>
<p>This narrates the curators' diligence ("individually tested", "gated", "complete
original test suite") — exactly the achievement framing standard 2 forbids, and "current
JDKs" is state that rots (standard 3). The intro and Lineage section already cover the
revival.</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> cut to one anonymous factual sentence, e.g. "The
build has been kept working on modern JDKs," and let the commit history carry the rest.</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">3</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">The Repository tour is studded with rot-prone measurements.</p>
<p><em>"~1,800 <code>.fss</code> test programs"</em>, <em>"53 components"</em>, <em>"the
4,518-line prelude"</em>, <em>"34 supporting scripts"</em>, <em>"1,071 generated AST
classes"</em>, <em>"the 2,125-line <code>Fortress.ast</code>"</em>, <em>"2&nbsp;MB"</em>.
Standard 3 violations, and some are already wrong: the tree holds 1,896 <code>.fss</code>
files today; <code>bin/</code> contains 34 entries <em>including</em> the launcher, so
"the launcher and 34 supporting scripts" is off by one. The prelude line count will rot
the day the planned complex-number work touches the library. The README itself links
<em>"the full, measured map"</em> in <code>repo-internals.md</code> — that is where
measurements belong.</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> strip every count; keep the qualitative
descriptions, which are good. ("Measured" in that closing sentence is also a faint
self-pat — "the full map" suffices.)</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">4</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">The famous quote is sourced against the repo's own research notes.</p>
<blockquote>Steele's ambition, as quoted in the 2005 announcement coverage, was to do
"for Fortran what Java did for C"</blockquote>
<p><code>research/extracts/fortress-websites-wayback.md</code> explicitly warns: the line
"does <strong>not</strong> appear on the archived project pages themselves — it is
attested in press coverage (e.g. HPCwire, JavaOne 2008 reports), so cite it as Sun press
framing, not as the site's own words." The README pins it to 2005 (unattested in the
repo) and puts the ambition in Steele's mouth. Standard 5, aggravated by contradicting
the repository's own sourcing discipline.</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> "the press framed the goal as doing 'for Fortran
what Java did for C'" — no year, no direct Steele attribution.</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">5</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">Spliced quotation.</p>
<blockquote>was "non-trivial — a euphemism for exponential cost."</blockquote>
<p>The extract records the slide as: <em>"'Non-trivial' is a euphemism for 'exponential
cost'"</em>. The README fuses a paraphrase inside quotation marks — words in an order
Steele didn't use. Standard 5 (quote fidelity).</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> quote exactly, or paraphrase outside quotation marks
and keep only <em>"So we had a grand vision, but could not quite pull it off"</em> as the
verbatim quote.</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">6</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">"Filed days after the final commits here" is precise and unsourced.</p>
<p>Nothing in the repo (research notes included) records the filing date of
US&nbsp;8,843,887. "Days after" invites falsification by anyone who opens the Google
Patents link. Standard 5.</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> verify the actual filing date before publishing, or
soften to "filed shortly after the wind-down."</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">7</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">"Direct descendant" overstates Steele's claim.</p>
<blockquote>Swift's optional binding (<code>if let</code>) as a direct descendant of
Fortress's <code>if x &lt;- z then … end</code></blockquote>
<p>Per the extract, slide 48's frame is ideas <em>hopping between languages</em> ("the
language that develops an idea may not be the one that survives") — a resemblance Steele
points to, not a documented causal lineage. Standard 5.</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> "points to Swift's <code>if let</code> as the same
idea resurfacing."</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">8</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">"The problem that ultimately stopped the project" over-claims causation —
and retells a story already told.</p>
<p>The research section's closing paragraph re-narrates the type-constraint impasse that
the History section ("2008–2012: the type-system frontier") just covered, and upgrades it
to the project's cause of death. The primary sources support "where it stalled" and a
corporate decision to conclude; they don't establish that this problem <em>stopped</em>
the project.</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> trim the paragraph to its genuinely new content —
that the unpublished drafts are the written record of the impasse, and the patent
afterlife — and drop "ultimately stopped."</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">9</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">"Rewritten in Scala starting February 2009" is falsified by the history
the README invites readers into.</p>
<p><code>git log</code> shows <code>scalasrc</code> created 2008-12-02 and
constraint-formula typechecker work committed 2008-12-24.
(<code>repo-internals.md</code> says "Feb 2009", but git is the primary source.)</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> "beginning in late 2008," or drop the date.</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">10</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">"The 1.0 specification and the reference interpreter followed in 2008."</p>
<p>The reference interpreter is <em>what was opened in January 2007</em> (the LICENSE's
own first line: "the Fortress reference interpreter… Copyright (c) 2007"). It did not
"follow" the open-sourcing.</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> "The 1.0 specification, with a matching interpreter
release, followed in March 2008."</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">11</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">The revival is stated three times.</p>
<p>Intro ("carried forward so that it still builds and runs…"), Building ("has been
modernized…"), Lineage ("the later migration work that keeps it running"). The curator's
standard is <em>one</em> terse anonymous note; three mentions read as quiet insistence.</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> keep the intro's single clause and the Lineage
sentence; excise the Building-section version (see finding 2).</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">12</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">Meta commentary.</p>
<p><em>"the research record (see above)"</em> in the Repository tour. Standard 4: the
document pointing at itself.</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> delete "(see above)" — the links already do the
work.</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">13</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">"still being absorbed by mainstream languages today"</p>
<p>"Today" anchors the sentence to the moment of writing (standard 3, mildly).</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> "ideas that mainstream languages have been absorbing
ever since."</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">14</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">Missing: not one line of Fortress is shown.</p>
<p>For an exhibit whose centerpiece is <em>what the language looked like</em>, the
catalog describes whiteboard-mathematics syntax at length but never displays it. A museum
catalog shows the artifact.</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> a short authentic fragment (a
<code>∑</code>-comprehension from the library, or four lines of
<code>buffons.fss</code>) between the intro and History would do more than all four
bullets.</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">15</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">Missing: the makers besides Steele.</p>
<p>The README's story is (correctly) the original authors, yet only Steele is named
outside paper citations. The repo's own wayback notes record "Sun Labs Programming
Language Research Group, principal investigators Guy L. Steele Jr. <strong>and Eric
Allen</strong>." A catalog credits the workshop, not only the master.</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> one sentence naming the PLRG and co-PI Allen in the
intro or History.</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">16</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">License nuance.</p>
<p><em>"Fortress is BSD-licensed"</em>: the LICENSE file opens "Unless otherwise noted
below…" and carries third-party exceptions. Minor over-flattening (standard 5).</p>
<p class="fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> "BSD-licensed, with third-party exceptions noted in
<code>LICENSE</code>."</p>
</div></li>

<li><span class="num">17</span><div class="finding">
<p class="claim">Small tics.</p>
<p>(a) <em>"<code>∑</code> and friends"</em> and later <em>"<code>typecheck</code>, and
friends"</em> — the same blog-casual idiom twice; keep at most one. (b) <em>"Top-level
<code>CompilerLibrary/</code> holds API stubs"</em> — "Top-level" is disambiguating
against a directory the reader hasn't been told about; either explain or drop the
qualifier. (c) <em>"(this repository's history begins 2007-01-04)"</em> — an ISO
timestamp mid-prose; "on 4 January 2007" fits the register better.</p>
</div></li>

</ol>

<h2>Overall fitness</h2>

<div class="verdict">
<p>The draft is much closer to an exhibition catalog than to an OSS pitch — the structure
(what it is → history → research → tour → running it → lineage) is exactly right, the
ordering puts the build instructions where the curator wants them, the paper inventory is
accurate and genuinely valuable, and the prose is mostly even and confident without being
promotional.</p>
<p>Its two systematic faults are (1) a residue of curator pride — the modernization
ladder paragraph, the false "tagged" promise, the inventory-audit numbers that say "look
how thoroughly we measured" — and (2) a handful of factual assertions pitched one notch
above what the primary sources support, several of which the repo's own research notes
explicitly caution against.</p>
<p>Fix findings 1–10 and the document will withstand exactly the kind of scrutiny its
subject — a historical artifact curated for skeptical visitors — will attract. The
remaining findings are polish.</p>
</div>
</main>

