<main>
<div class="eyebrow">Fortress revival · goal 6 · feasibility map</div>
<h1>microgpt.fss — what we have, what's missing</h1>

<div class="banner"><strong>Verdict: feasible on the interpreter, no blockers found.</strong> Every language feature and nearly every library piece Karpathy's microgpt needs already exists in the 2012 tree; the gaps are three small routines and one design adaptation, all writable in .fss. The single real unknown is interpreter throughput — measured first, before any porting (step 0).</div>

<p><strong>Target:</strong> Karpathy's <a href="http://karpathy.github.io/2026/02/12/microgpt/">microgpt</a> (Feb 2026) — a complete GPT in 243 lines of dependency-free Python: scalar reverse-mode autodiff (<code>Value</code>, six operations with their local gradients), char-level tokenizer, a 1-layer / 4-head transformer (rmsnorm, attention with KV cache, relu MLP), Adam with bias correction and linear LR decay, temperature sampling. Trains on makemore's <code>names.txt</code> and hallucinates new names. Full source recovered from the post.</p>

<p><strong>Why this target:</strong> the scalar formulation needs no tensors and no numpy — it exercises exactly what Fortress is good at (objects, operator overloading, mathematical notation) and nothing we lack. The port becomes the notation twin: the same program, but the forward and backward math typesets via Fortify. At Karpathy's defaults: vocab ≈ 28, n_embd 16, block 16 ⇒ ≈ 4,200 parameters; ~10⁵ graph nodes per step, 1,000 steps ⇒ order 10⁸ scalar graph operations for the full run.</p>

<h2>Language features</h2>
<div class="tablewrap">
<table>
<tr><th>microgpt needs</th><th>Fortress</th><th></th></tr>
<tr><td>class with mutable fields (<code>Value.data/.grad</code>)</td><td><code>object</code> with <code>var</code> fields</td><td><span class="chip have">have</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>operator overloading (<code>+ · / aⁿ neg</code>)</td><td><code>opr</code> methods — core strength</td><td><span class="chip have">have</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>closures, local recursive fn (<code>build_topo</code>)</td><td><code>fn</code> lambdas, local functions</td><td><span class="chip have">have</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>comprehensions, <code>zip</code>, slicing <code>q[a:b]</code></td><td><code>&lt;|...|&gt;</code>, <code>List.zip</code>, range indexing <code>a[lo#len]</code></td><td><span class="chip have">have</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>sequential loops (training is stateful)</td><td><code>for i &lt;- seq(...)</code> — Fortress loops are parallel by default</td><td><span class="chip have">have</span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Library</h2>
<div class="tablewrap">
<table>
<tr><th>microgpt needs</th><th>Library has</th><th></th></tr>
<tr><td>growable list, <code>reversed</code></td><td><code>List</code>: addRight, reverse, zip, indexOf; arrays for hot buffers</td><td><span class="chip have">have</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>dict <code>state_dict</code></td><td><code>Map</code> exists — but named fields in a model object are better Fortress</td><td><span class="chip have">have</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>random.shuffle</code></td><td><code>Shuffle.shuffle</code> (list + array, fairness-documented)</td><td><span class="chip have">have</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>uniform random</td><td><code>random(a):RR64</code>, <code>randomZZ32</code> natives (buffons uses them)</td><td><span class="chip have">have</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>math.log/exp</code>, <code>** 0.5</code></td><td><code>RR64.log/exp</code>, <code>^</code> (realArith-tested)</td><td><span class="chip have">have</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>read <code>input.txt</code> lines</td><td><code>FileReadStream.lines()</code> (FileReadWrite tests)</td><td><span class="chip have">have</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>chars of a string, <code>uchars.index(ch)</code></td><td>String generates <code>Char</code>; <code>indexOf</code></td><td><span class="chip have">have</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>random.gauss</code> init</td><td>Box–Muller from <code>random()</code>, ~4 lines</td><td><span class="chip gap">write</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>random.choices</code> (weighted sampling)</td><td>CDF scan over the probs, ~5 lines</td><td><span class="chip gap">write</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>identity-based visited set in <code>backward()</code></td><td><code>opr SEQV</code> exists but no identity-hashed Set → <code>var visited</code> flag on the node (or epoch counter)</td><td><span class="chip gap">adapt</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>vocabulary sort (~30 chars)</td><td>library sort unverified; worst case inline insertion sort</td><td><span class="chip verify">verify</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>%.4f</code>-style loss printing</td><td><code>Format</code> library — check coverage</td><td><span class="chip verify">verify</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>names.txt</code></td><td>fetch/commit separately (32k lines, makemore corpus)</td><td><span class="chip verify">decide</span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Types — the direct answer</h2>
<p>The port needs <code>RR64</code> scalars, <code>ZZ32</code> indices, <code>String</code>/<code>Char</code>, <code>Boolean</code>, and one user object type. All exist; the numeric tower and ZZ32→RR64 coercions are exercised by the whole test suite. No tensors are involved anywhere — that's the point of the scalar formulation — so the known library gaps (no ℂ, no linear algebra, the <code>incomplete/</code> directory) never come into play. A typed-tensor, shape-checked showcase is a separate, later étude on Fortress's dimension-typed arrays; microgpt needs none of it.</p>

<h2>Baseline (measured here, 2026-08-23)</h2>
<p>Original assembled from the post and run in this container (CPython 3.11.15, 4-core Xeon 2.10 GHz, seeded): 32,033 docs, vocab 27, <strong>4,192 params</strong>; <strong>1000 steps + 20 samples in 120.3 s ≈ 0.12 s/step</strong>; per-doc loss 3.3660 → ~2.3 band; samples convincingly name-like (kamon, keylen, alerin, anton…). The CPython bar is ~2 minutes — the port needn't beat it, matching within an order of magnitude on the interpreter would be strong.</p>
<p><strong>Correctness anchors</strong> (the original ships no tests): golden activations — fixed deterministic weights, fixed token sequence, logits + gradients dumped from Python to ~12 digits, asserted against the Fortress forward/backward (RNG-independent, exact); plus a statistical loss-trajectory band (~3.37 falling into ~2.3) under the port's own RNG.</p>
<p><strong>Walk vs. compile:</strong> interpreter-only, realistically — the compiled path's CompilerLibrary is a 592-line skeleton vs. the interpreter's 4,518-line FortressLibrary (no List/Map/Shuffle/files/Random). Compiling microgpt is a stretch target gated on goal 4, not the plan.</p>

<h2>Risks</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Interpreter throughput</strong> — the only real unknown. At 10⁵–10⁶ scalar graph ops/sec the full 1,000-step run is minutes to hours; an order slower and it needs patience or a smaller config. Step 0 measures before anything is built.</li>
<li><strong>Recursion depth</strong> — <code>build_topo</code> recurses to graph <em>depth</em> (longest chain, hundreds), not node count; if the Java stack ever objects, <code>-Xss</code> or a work-list version fixes it.</li>
<li><strong>List performance</strong> — the library <code>List</code> is a functional deque; fine at this scale, arrays as fallback for hot buffers.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Executed so far (2026-08-23)</h2>
<p><strong>Step 0 measured</strong> (<code>explorations/mgbench.fss</code>): raw scalar loop ~45k iter/s; Value-node build ~10k nodes/s; backward walk ~2.8k nodes/s; Array ≈ List for dots (interpreter overhead dominates). Projection at Karpathy config: <strong>~20 s/step, ~5–6 h full run</strong> (~170× CPython) — so development happens in a nano config (~5 s/step) and the dot-fusion lever (one Value per dot product, ~16× fewer nodes) is promoted from reserve to likely, after golden verification.</p>
<p><strong>Stage 1 done</strong> (<code>explorations/micrograd.fss</code>): the Value autodiff object, visited-flag topo sort, analytic golden gradient checks (all PASS), and a 2→4→1 relu MLP trained on XOR to 5.5e-11 MSE. <strong>Headline: Fortress and Python trajectories are bit-for-bit identical</strong> — a deterministic Python twin prints the same loss to all 16 digits at every checkpoint, including a first run where both collapsed to the same dead-relu failure and were fixed by the same bias-init change. Goldens for +/·/relu graphs can therefore demand exact equality; ulp tolerance is needed only for exp/log/pow.</p>

<h2>Staging</h2>
<ol class="seq">
<li><strong>Microbenchmark</strong> — build ~10⁵ <code>Value</code> nodes, run <code>backward()</code>, time it. Decides the feasible config before any porting.</li>
<li><strong>micrograd.fss</strong> — <code>Value</code> + <code>backward()</code> + a tiny MLP on a toy task. Settles the object-model and notation questions cheaply; the six-operation "lego blocks" table becomes six <code>opr</code> methods.</li>
<li><strong>Transformer forward pass</strong> — one block, golden-tested against reference activations dumped from the Python.</li>
<li><strong>Full microgpt.fss</strong> — training on (a subset of) names.txt; loss curve down, sampled names out.</li>
<li><strong>Fortify rendering</strong> — the listing typeset as mathematics; the executable-paper artifact.</li>
</ol>

<p class="note">Committed as <code>explorations/microgpt-port.md</code>; goal 6 in CLAUDE.md. Source blocks recovered to the session scratchpad.</p>
</main>

